<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#zamorin">Glossary</a> · Term · title</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zamorin</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Samoothiri</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Zamorin, in Malayalam Samoothiri, was the title of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> rulers of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calicut">Calicut</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malabar-coast">Malabar Coast</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a> from about the 12th century to 1766. Growing rich on the spice trade with Arab and other Muslim merchants, the Zamorins received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasco-da-gama">Vasco da Gama</a> in 1498 and fought the Portuguese for much of the 16th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyder-ali">Hyder Ali</a>'s invasion of 1766 ended their independence, though the family survives.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zamorin.jpg" alt="Zamorin" width="320" height="368" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Veloso Salgado · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zamorin_of_Calicut.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
